1
00:00:08,675 --> 00:00:12,734
[Subtitles by danielsangeo]

2
00:00:13,733 --> 00:00:14,567
Ahem...

3
00:00:15,220 --> 00:00:18,218
Awoooooooooooooooooooooooooooooooo!

4
00:00:18,218 --> 00:00:20,019
Wait, what the hell am I doing?

5
00:00:20,019 --> 00:00:21,937
That's right, I was going to get out of here.

6
00:00:21,937 --> 00:00:24,133
The moon's a secondary objective.

7
00:00:24,133 --> 00:00:26,034
God, I still can't believe it's night out.

8
00:00:26,034 --> 00:00:28,108
It only felt like a couple hours.

9
00:00:28,108 --> 00:00:30,182
Dammit, everything's like this now.

10
00:00:30,182 --> 00:00:32,877
People always say, "Time flies
when you're having fun,"

11
00:00:32,877 --> 00:00:36,781
well, what happens is that time just
flies, period, and we all end up older.

12
00:00:36,781 --> 00:00:41,213
All right. Let me see, maybe I could
pry this door open, I dunno.

13
00:00:41,213 --> 00:00:42,361
What's this?

14
00:00:42,361 --> 00:00:44,656
"Extreme Heat - Clear Area".

15
00:00:44,656 --> 00:00:47,628
Yeah, right, this is probably just
where the department has barbecues

16
00:00:47,628 --> 00:00:49,589
and they put up that
sign to make it private.

17
00:00:49,589 --> 00:00:52,091
I know how people think around here.

18
00:00:52,091 --> 00:00:53,758
Hey, a keypad.

19
00:00:53,758 --> 00:00:55,400
I don't know the code.

20
00:00:56,078 --> 00:00:57,568
I'll try some random numbers.

21
00:00:57,568 --> 00:00:58,419
[buttons beeping]

22
00:00:58,419 --> 00:00:59,133
No.

23
00:00:59,804 --> 00:01:01,881
Okay, 1-3-3-7.

24
00:01:01,881 --> 00:01:02,740
No.

25
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:03,801
1-2-3-4.

26
00:01:04,682 --> 00:01:06,450
Ah-ha-ha!

27
00:01:06,450 --> 00:01:11,364
You know, as much as I'd like to claim
this is the result of me being a genius,

28
00:01:11,364 --> 00:01:14,488
it's more that someone else was not.

29
00:01:14,488 --> 00:01:17,587
We probably spent tens of
millions on this security system

30
00:01:17,587 --> 00:01:20,466
then paid our janitor
minimum wage to install it.

31
00:01:20,466 --> 00:01:23,696
Knowing this code makes me a
threat to national security now.

32
00:01:23,696 --> 00:01:24,634
[HECU chatter]

33
00:01:25,406 --> 00:01:26,378
SPEAKING OF THREATS...!

34
00:01:26,378 --> 00:01:27,154
HECU: Recon!

35
00:01:28,385 --> 00:01:29,528
Whoa!

36
00:01:29,528 --> 00:01:30,133
He just--

37
00:01:32,162 --> 00:01:33,880
Did I see that right?

38
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:37,486
That guy in the beret just shot
a grenade round at his buddies there.

39
00:01:37,486 --> 00:01:41,739
I don't think he was even aiming
for me; I was just the excuse.

40
00:01:41,739 --> 00:01:44,994
I mean, what is this?
"The Three Stooges Join the Corps"?

41
00:01:44,994 --> 00:01:47,045
There are three of them.

42
00:01:47,045 --> 00:01:48,685
This is crazy.

43
00:01:48,685 --> 00:01:52,488
Not that I'm complaining about
the friendly-fire, but, wow!

44
00:01:52,488 --> 00:01:56,049
I always expected this sort of
thing to happen to the Stooges actually.

45
00:01:56,049 --> 00:01:58,053
That one day Moe would
just fucking lose it

46
00:01:58,053 --> 00:02:00,656
and kill the other two
Stooges for incompetence.

47
00:02:00,656 --> 00:02:03,758
Well, maybe not Larry, he could
be brought back into the fold.

48
00:02:03,758 --> 00:02:05,285
But definitely Curly.

49
00:02:05,285 --> 00:02:07,601
Curly Joe was beyond redemption.

50
00:02:10,158 --> 00:02:11,762
Any more Stooges?!

51
00:02:11,762 --> 00:02:13,516
Shemp, is that you?!

52
00:02:13,516 --> 00:02:15,052
No, just three.

53
00:02:15,052 --> 00:02:18,367
Oh, good, bloody handprint. Now
I know I'm going the right way again.

54
00:02:19,075 --> 00:02:24,002
Whoa... that's a lot of
explosives there.

55
00:02:24,002 --> 00:02:25,645
And tripwires...

56
00:02:25,645 --> 00:02:29,214
This is like that joke where you
balance a bucket of water on a door,

57
00:02:29,214 --> 00:02:33,111
but, as usual, the military here
has to overdo everything.

58
00:02:33,111 --> 00:02:36,608
Well, whatever's in that door is
obviously not worth keeping.

59
00:02:36,608 --> 00:02:42,234
Oh, so maybe that wasn't a guiding bloody
handprint but a warning bloody handprint.

60
00:02:43,018 --> 00:02:45,746
I don't know, I just can't
tell the difference.

61
00:02:45,746 --> 00:02:47,234
Hey, there's an open window.

62
00:02:49,551 --> 00:02:50,202
Why would they--

63
00:02:50,202 --> 00:02:52,924
HECU: Recon team check in.

64
00:02:52,924 --> 00:02:55,033
Well, that's not what I wanted to hear.

65
00:02:55,923 --> 00:02:58,547
I don't know, I think there
might be something good in here.

66
00:02:58,547 --> 00:03:01,167
The path of blood hasn't
let me down so far.

67
00:03:02,338 --> 00:03:06,932
HECU: Command host team
in position, over.

68
00:03:06,932 --> 00:03:10,213
These guys sound pretty chill considering
there were gunshots and an explosion

69
00:03:10,213 --> 00:03:12,168
outside not two minutes earlier.

70
00:03:12,168 --> 00:03:15,263
I guess it doesn't occur to
them to investigate that.

71
00:03:15,263 --> 00:03:17,865
And now we return to
Ethical Dilemma Theater.

72
00:03:17,865 --> 00:03:20,396
Do I shoot them... or not?

73
00:03:20,396 --> 00:03:24,025
I mean, those other guys were making it
pretty clear they did not like me,

74
00:03:24,025 --> 00:03:26,605
which is a capital offense, I guess.

75
00:03:26,605 --> 00:03:30,133
But these guys are just saying,
"Alpha Bravo Position Flanker Gamma Delta--"

76
00:03:30,133 --> 00:03:33,209
HECU: Squad, all hostiles clear, over.

77
00:03:33,209 --> 00:03:35,209
[HECU chatter overlapping]
Yeah, see, this is what I'm talking about.

78
00:03:35,209 --> 00:03:35,846
[HECU chatter overlapping]

79
00:03:35,846 --> 00:03:37,680
[HECU chatter overlapping]
Okay, you can stop.

80
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:39,798
[HECU chatter overlapping]
Guys, I can't even hear myself think.

81
00:03:39,798 --> 00:03:40,934
[HECU chatter overlapping]
Wait, what am I doing?

82
00:03:40,934 --> 00:03:42,067
HECU: SQUAD, WE GOT FREEMAN!
SHIT!

83
00:03:42,956 --> 00:03:43,980
Daaah!

84
00:03:46,021 --> 00:03:49,381
Oh, God, those shots were close to my head!

85
00:03:49,381 --> 00:03:52,896
Why can't they aim for my impenetrable
body armor like everybody else?

86
00:03:52,896 --> 00:03:55,797
One of those shots ricocheted off my collar!

87
00:03:55,797 --> 00:03:59,012
YOU GUYS COULD HAVE KILLED ME,
YOU KNOW THAT?!

88
00:03:59,012 --> 00:04:01,198
Well, I guess this guy knows it.

89
00:04:01,198 --> 00:04:04,003
That's it, no more Mr. Nice Guy.

90
00:04:04,003 --> 00:04:08,061
I keep clinging onto the hope that
ONE of these goons will hear me out,

91
00:04:08,061 --> 00:04:09,612
but it just isn't happening.

92
00:04:09,612 --> 00:04:13,916
This self-defense crap isn't cutting it,
because I never get to fire first.

93
00:04:13,916 --> 00:04:15,932
Damn, no, not this way.

94
00:04:15,932 --> 00:04:18,990
Normally, I'd need a really good
reason to murder someone,

95
00:04:18,990 --> 00:04:20,970
but this whole day's been fucked.

96
00:04:20,970 --> 00:04:23,910
Better than a hundred
probably-guilty people die

97
00:04:23,910 --> 00:04:26,414
than one of them get a clean shot on me.

98
00:04:26,414 --> 00:04:28,291
I mean, look at all these dead scientists.

99
00:04:28,291 --> 00:04:30,787
This rescue mission's a disaster anyway.

100
00:04:30,787 --> 00:04:34,234
500 dead, 1200 dead, who's counting?

101
00:04:34,726 --> 00:04:36,369
All right, where am I?

102
00:04:36,369 --> 00:04:37,938
Oh, I know what this is.

103
00:04:37,938 --> 00:04:40,400
Those are electron
microscope shots of cells.

104
00:04:41,047 --> 00:04:43,149
Yeah, that one's pollen, I think.

105
00:04:43,149 --> 00:04:45,287
This must be the Microbiology Department.

106
00:04:45,287 --> 00:04:48,037
Why does the military want to
kill microbiologists?

107
00:04:48,037 --> 00:04:51,758
I've never really known them to
be a controversial bunch.

108
00:04:51,758 --> 00:04:53,592
I think some people yelled at Pasteur

109
00:04:53,592 --> 00:04:57,757
because they didn't understand what vaccines
were, but that's all that comes to mind.

110
00:04:57,757 --> 00:05:01,956
But why do microbiologists need
this very expensive hologram projector?

111
00:05:01,956 --> 00:05:03,401
We didn't get one of those.

112
00:05:03,401 --> 00:05:04,982
We could've used it more than they could.

113
00:05:04,982 --> 00:05:07,377
What's that say? Something about satellites?

114
00:05:07,377 --> 00:05:08,705
They don't need this.

115
00:05:08,705 --> 00:05:11,273
They're padding their costs to
get more grant money.

116
00:05:11,273 --> 00:05:14,082
Well, damn, maybe they did do
something to get shot.

117
00:05:14,082 --> 00:05:17,010
Pissed off the wrong general
with these costs.

118
00:05:17,010 --> 00:05:19,583
And that's the attitude I need
to start taking.

119
00:05:19,583 --> 00:05:20,935
Just kill everyone.

120
00:05:20,935 --> 00:05:23,294
If you're wearing a military
uniform, you're dead.

121
00:05:23,294 --> 00:05:27,006
If somebody's grandma is cold and
puts on a camo blanket, she's dead.

122
00:05:27,006 --> 00:05:29,114
I need to just be a psychopath.

123
00:05:29,114 --> 00:05:30,267
Hey, what's this do?

124
00:05:30,682 --> 00:05:33,482
[alarms blare]

125
00:05:33,482 --> 00:05:34,076
[alarms blare]
Uh oh.

126
00:05:34,076 --> 00:05:35,288
[alarms blare]

127
00:05:35,288 --> 00:05:36,357
[alarms blare]
Uh...

128
00:05:36,357 --> 00:05:37,453
[roaring sound]
Maybe I should stop

129
00:05:37,453 --> 00:05:39,122
[roaring sound]
pressing every button I see.

130
00:05:39,122 --> 00:05:43,667
[roaring sound]

131
00:05:43,667 --> 00:05:45,829
[roaring sound]
JESUS CHRIST, I LAUNCHED A MISSILE!

132
00:05:45,829 --> 00:05:48,943
[roaring sound]

133
00:05:48,943 --> 00:05:51,112
[roaring sound]
I'm not helping anything!

134
00:05:51,112 --> 00:05:54,971
[roaring sound]

135
00:05:54,971 --> 00:05:56,907
[roaring sound]
AUGH! MY EYES!

136
00:05:56,907 --> 00:05:59,703
[roaring sound]

137
00:05:59,703 --> 00:06:03,429
[roaring sound]
GAZE UPON THE FIERY DOOM OF THIS EARTH...!

138
00:06:03,429 --> 00:06:06,218
[roaring sound fades]

139
00:06:06,218 --> 00:06:09,534
So, guess I just started World War III.

140
00:06:10,010 --> 00:06:11,367
This been a busy day.

141
00:06:11,986 --> 00:06:15,008
But, in the end, there's only
one thing that matters:

142
00:06:15,008 --> 00:06:17,834
I did not leave any
fingerprints.

143
00:06:18,405 --> 00:06:20,560
I was wearing my suit.

144
00:06:20,560 --> 00:06:21,749
I'm getting out of here.

145
00:06:27,626 --> 00:06:28,748
Yeah...

146
00:06:29,825 --> 00:06:32,014
I don't always know when
I'm in over my head,

147
00:06:32,014 --> 00:06:35,225
but I'm pretty sure this is one of those times.

148
00:06:35,225 --> 00:06:37,764
Oh, and what's this?

149
00:06:37,764 --> 00:06:42,429
Could this be a ridged pipe that
leads straight up the rock face?

150
00:06:42,429 --> 00:06:43,901
I think it is.

151
00:06:46,611 --> 00:06:47,200
Ah.

152
00:06:48,428 --> 00:06:51,033
That's right, I'm in New Mexico, aren't I?

153
00:06:51,752 --> 00:06:53,400
The middle of the desert.

154
00:06:54,228 --> 00:06:57,367
The middle of the desert.

155
00:06:58,185 --> 00:07:03,067
Now that I think about it, the flight in here
might have been longer than I remember.

156
00:07:03,825 --> 00:07:06,934
So, if I were to pack it on
foot, how far could I go?

157
00:07:07,673 --> 00:07:10,913
If I had food and water--which I don't--

158
00:07:10,913 --> 00:07:14,343
I could go twenty, maybe
thirty miles in a day

159
00:07:14,343 --> 00:07:16,295
assuming the sun didn't beat me down.

160
00:07:16,295 --> 00:07:17,291
Which it would.

161
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:20,834
I really should have thought about this sooner.

162
00:07:20,834 --> 00:07:24,367
Okay, so maybe walking out is
not the way to go here.

163
00:07:25,134 --> 00:07:26,500
Dammit.

164
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:29,187
All right, I'm going to have find a car.

165
00:07:29,187 --> 00:07:32,711
God, the fun just doesn't
end today, does it?

166
00:07:32,711 --> 00:07:35,238
Well, that's not a problem. I can cope.

167
00:07:35,238 --> 00:07:37,943
I'm going to kill everyone.

168
00:07:37,943 --> 00:07:41,261
It's not even going to be about
stress-relief, that's a side-benefit.

169
00:07:41,261 --> 00:07:42,879
This is survival.

170
00:07:42,879 --> 00:07:45,574
People say you have to play the
cards you've been dealt in life.

171
00:07:45,574 --> 00:07:46,687
I don't think so.

172
00:07:46,687 --> 00:07:48,616
I think if you've been dealt
a stacked deck,

173
00:07:48,616 --> 00:07:50,062
you can knock the table over,

174
00:07:50,062 --> 00:07:52,655
pull out a gun and start firing on...

175
00:07:52,655 --> 00:07:55,133
life... I guess... I don't know.

176
00:07:57,817 --> 00:07:59,291
[crickets chirp]

177
00:07:59,291 --> 00:08:02,303
[crickets chirp]
Huh. This must be some pretty jaded crickets to

178
00:08:02,303 --> 00:08:05,175
[crickets chirp]
keep on chirping after that missile launch.

179
00:08:05,175 --> 00:08:07,985
[crickets chirp]
Wait a minute, this wasn't open.

180
00:08:07,985 --> 00:08:10,058
[crickets chirp]
And somebody moved the bodies.

181
00:08:10,058 --> 00:08:11,937
[crickets chirp]
Okay, I've seen enough.

182
00:08:11,937 --> 00:08:13,444
[crickets chirp]
With any luck, whoever was here

183
00:08:13,444 --> 00:08:15,139
[crickets chirp]
went back into the endless tram tunnel

184
00:08:15,139 --> 00:08:16,767
[crickets chirp]
and will be there a few days.

185
00:08:18,985 --> 00:08:22,196
Oh, no, no, no.
Is this the only path left?

186
00:08:22,196 --> 00:08:25,061
Not down. I don't do down anymore.

187
00:08:25,061 --> 00:08:27,923
I've renounced my status as
King of the Underworld.

188
00:08:28,752 --> 00:08:31,300
Well, I'm not going down there,
so that's the end of it.

189
00:08:33,788 --> 00:08:34,300
Hm.

190
00:08:34,970 --> 00:08:39,067
I think a tram-based pizza delivery
system can make a lot of sense.

